            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors, and around the attic access hatch. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

Air leakage was observed along the basebaords and corner within the kitchen, which I suspect is the reason these cupboards are quite cold during the winter. 

Air leakage was also observed through the bathroom exhaust fan. I recommend you replace your exhaust fan with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.

The attic access hatch had quite a bit of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $ 120 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.6 GJ, and qualify for up to$ 500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



Additionally: It was mentioned that the open fireplace is not use, and you have no future plans of using it. Ideally this would be removed completely, however the air leakage can be addressed by plugging the flue from both the top and bottom. It is currently filled with fiberglass batts, which are not great in terms of blocking air flow. By blocking off the flue, you could potentially save an additional $ 170 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.7 GJ.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R20) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



Savings:

By adding R20 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $ 800 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 23.3 GJ, and qualify for up to$ 4,000 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for an insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was assumed to have 3.5 inches (R8) of wood shavings, as well as 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation, I have modelled adding 9 inches (R30) of blown cellulose insulation for a total insulation value of R50.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R50, you could potentially save $ 380 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 375 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for ceiling insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a ceiling insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</ceilings>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, they are however in reasonable shape. 

I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the three remaing wood framed windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing the three wood framed windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $ 300 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.8 GJ.  Windows that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified and rated for climate zone 3 are eligible for $ 250 per window in rebates, to a max of 12 windows through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil-fired boiler was originally rated at 85.3% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 1,300 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 32.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for the boiler will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a boiler rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DOMESTIC HOT WATER

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.

As you currently have a very efficient oil boiler (and a loop is already in place to the hot water tank), it would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water year round, and simply turn down the thermostats around the home during the summer months. 



In conjuction with the heating upgrade mentioned above, a boiler upgrade will also increase the efficiency of the combi loop used to heat your domestic hot water. 



Savings:

By upgrading your boiler to a 95% condensing propane unit, and continuing to heat your hot water with it, you could potentially save $ 110 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.7 GJ.  </HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

COMBUSTION AIR

I did not see a combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the boiler. Some combustion gas could be smelt in the entrance area whenever the boiler fired-up. 

I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your boiler is satisfied. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boiler is operating.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



THERMOSTATS

You mentioned the bedrooms/back of the house becomes quite cool, compared to the kitchen and living rooms areas. Due to the proximity of the two thermostats, I suspect these are "fighting" with each other. The thermostat in the kitchen may be acting as the 'primary' heating loop, which heats the kitchen and living room areas. 



As the hallway (where the second thermostat is located) is right behind the living room area, I suspect this thermostat is being affected by the 'primary' heating loop, thinking the heat is satisfied due to the close proximity. Therefore the 'secondary' loop, which heats the bathroom and bedrooms, is rarely turning on. 



By moving one of the thermostats to another location, this may solve the uneven heating issue you've encountered. 

Another option to consider upgrading your thermostats. Many smart programmable thermostats have the ability to add multiple wireless temperature sensors anywhere around the home. The Arctic Energy Alliance offers rebates of 25% of the purchase cost, up to a maximum of $ 75, for programmable thermostats. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

